## What is IIIF?

IIIF (International Image Interoperability Framework, pronounced “triple-eye-eff”)
is a set of open standards for sharing images and other digital collections on
the web. Libraries, archives and museums use IIIF to make their collections
accessible across different tools and websites.

Because Allmaps supports IIIF, it can load maps directly from these institutions
without you needing to download or upload the images.

Read <a href="{docsBaseUrl}/iiif">our introduction to IIIF</a> or visit the
[IIIF website](https://iiif.io/) to learn more.

## I still don’t understand -- I just want to look at maps!

Browse recently georeferenced maps in <a href={toolIdentities.latest.href}>{toolIdentities.latest.name}</a>,
or discover historical maps around your current location with
<a href={toolIdentities.here.href}>{toolIdentities.here.name}</a>. Open a map in
<a href={toolIdentities.viewer.href}>{toolIdentities.viewer.name}</a> to explore it over a modern map.

## Can I upload my own maps?

No. Allmaps works with maps that are already published through IIIF by libraries,
archives and other repositories. You provide a IIIF URL, and the images stay with
the institution or service hosting them.

## Does Allmaps create map tiles or GeoTIFFs?

Allmaps can display georeferenced maps directly in your browser without first
creating map tiles or GeoTIFFs. It loads the original IIIF images and transforms
them on demand.

If you need maps in other tools, Allmaps Tile Server provides XYZ map tiles, and
you can generate GeoTIFFs using Allmaps and GDAL. See our
<a href="{docsBaseUrl}/guides/using-maps">guide to using maps</a> for the available
options.


