## Can I display georeferenced maps on my own website?

<p>
  Yes. Allmaps provides plugins for
  <a href={`${docsBaseUrl}/packages/maplibre`}>MapLibre</a>,
  <a href={`${docsBaseUrl}/packages/openlayers`}>OpenLayers</a> and
  <a href={`${docsBaseUrl}/packages/leaflet`}>Leaflet</a>, so you can add
  georeferenced maps to your own website.
</p>

The plugins load images directly from IIIF servers and use Georeference
Annotations to position and transform them in the browser.

<p class="mt-6">
  Explore interactive examples and code for all three plugins in our
  <a href={`${docsBaseUrl}/guides/viewing-maps#try-the-plugins`}>guide to viewing maps</a>.
</p>

</div>

<div class="not-prose min-w-0">
  <PluginPreview compact />
</div>
</section>

## Can I use georeferenced maps in books or presentations?

Yes. You can create PNG, JPEG or WebP images of georeferenced maps for books,
articles, presentations and other publications.

Our <a href="{docsBaseUrl}/guides/using-maps#png-jpeg-and-webp">guide to exporting map images</a>
lets you choose the image dimensions and download the result directly in your
browser. Developers can also use the
<a href="{docsBaseUrl}/packages/render">Allmaps render module</a> to generate
images in their own projects.

## Is Allmaps free to use?

Allmaps’ software and open georeference data are free to use, including in
commercial projects, under their respective licenses.

The hosted Allmaps APIs, including Allmaps Tile Server, are currently free to
use under our [fair use policy](/terms#fair-use-of-hosted-apis). See our
[Terms & Usage](/terms) for details.

## Is Allmaps open source?

Yes. The whole Allmaps project is open source, including its web applications,
APIs, libraries and plugins. You can find the code on
[GitHub](https://github.com/allmaps/allmaps), use it in your own projects and
contribute improvements.

## Can I host Allmaps myself?

Allmaps is open source, so you can run the software yourself. However, we
recommend using the hosted Allmaps Editor and APIs. Keeping contributions
together in one shared database helps everyone discover and reuse georeferenced
maps across institutions.

We don’t actively provide support for setting up separate Allmaps instances.
If you want to build your own tools or websites, our open source libraries,
plugins and open data are designed for reuse.

## Who is behind Allmaps?

Allmaps was started by Bert Spaan and Jules Schoonman. The project is developed
together with freelancers, partner institutions and contributors from the wider
IIIF community.

Read more about [the people and ideas behind Allmaps](/about).

## How is Allmaps funded?

Allmaps is funded through grants, commissioned work and support from partner
institutions. The IIIF–Allmaps Partnership gives members of the IIIF Consortium
a way to support ongoing development and maintenance through their membership.

Learn more about the [IIIF–Allmaps Partnership](/iiif-partnership).

